        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 290.2067083333334,
            "to": 300.2067083333334
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A student reading a paediatric chest X-ray notices cardiomegaly but skips the bo</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A student reading a paediatric chest X-ray notices cardiomegaly but skips the bone review. Which diagnosis could this oversight cause them to miss?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Atrial septal defect</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Atrial septal defect does not produce rib notching; it may show increased pulmonary vascular markings, but bone review would not specifically reveal it.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Patent ductus arteriosus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>PDA does not cause characteristic bony changes. Its radiographic signs are pulmonary plethora and left atrial enlargement, not rib abnormalities.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Coarctation of the aorta</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Rib notching — caused by dilated intercostal collateral arteries — is a classic radiographic sign of coarctation. Skipping the bone check in the ABCDE framework means missing this finding.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pulmonary stenosis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Pulmonary stenosis may show a prominent pulmonary artery segment on X-ray but is not associated with rib notching.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The narration warned that skipping the bone check means missing rib notching — a classic sign of coarctation of the aorta. The systematic ABCDE approach prevents exactly this kind of tunnel vision."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! The ABCDE framework ensures you check bones before focusing on the cardiac shadow. Rib notching is pathognomonic of coarctation of the aorta and is easily missed when the reader jumps straight to the heart."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
