          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A seven-year-old child presents with fever, migratory polyarthritis, and a new a</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A seven-year-old child presents with fever, migratory polyarthritis, and a new apical pansystolic murmur three weeks after a sore throat. Which cardiac valve is most likely affected?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Aortic valve</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The aortic valve can be involved in rheumatic carditis, but it is less commonly affected than the mitral valve. Aortic regurgitation produces an early diastolic murmur along the left sternal border, not an apical pansystolic murmur.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Tricuspid valve</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Right-sided valves, including the tricuspid, are almost never involved in rheumatic carditis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pulmonary valve</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Pulmonary valve involvement in acute rheumatic fever is extremely rare. Right-sided valves are characteristically spared.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Mitral valve</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The mitral valve is the most commonly and most severely affected valve in rheumatic carditis. The resulting mitral regurgitation produces a pansystolic murmur best heard at the apex, radiating to the axilla.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The narration stated that the mitral valve is 'hit hardest' in rheumatic carditis, creating a murmur heard at the apex and spreading towards the axilla. Right-sided valves are almost never involved."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! In acute rheumatic fever, the mitral valve bears the brunt of valvular inflammation. The apical pansystolic murmur of mitral regurgitation, radiating to the axilla, is the most characteristic cardiac finding."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
