          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A six-month-old infant with a large ventricular septal defect is in cardiac fail</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A six-month-old infant with a large ventricular septal defect is in cardiac failure. Which combination of medications forms the cornerstone of initial medical management?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Aspirin, prednisolone, and benzathine penicillin</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This combination is used for treating acute rheumatic fever, not for managing cardiac failure from a congenital left-to-right shunt.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Furosemide, digoxin, and captopril</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Furosemide reduces fluid overload, digoxin improves cardiac contractility, and captopril lowers afterload — together they stabilise the failing infant heart while awaiting timely surgical repair.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Indomethacin, surfactant, and dopamine</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Indomethacin is used specifically to close a patent ductus arteriosus in premature neonates. This combination addresses neonatal respiratory and circulatory support, not VSD-related heart failure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Atenolol, amlodipine, and enalapril</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>These are antihypertensive agents predominantly used in older patients and are not the standard initial regimen for managing cardiac failure from a large VSD in infancy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The narration specified furosemide (diuretic to reduce fluid overload), digoxin (to improve cardiac contractility), and captopril (to lower afterload) as the medical management triad for infants with large shunt lesions."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! The narration described furosemide, digoxin, and captopril as the key medications for infants in heart failure from large left-to-right shunts, with all doses strictly weight-based in children."
                }
              ],
